WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.380
Hi.

2
00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:02.270
Hi.

3
00:00:02.270 --> 00:00:06.610
I just want you to know that I watched the documentary,

4
00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:10.020
and there was so much stuff I related to.

5
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:10.853
Yeah.

6
00:00:10.853 --> 00:00:13.110
And I'm really excited

7
00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:17.000
that I might get the chance to talk to somebody

8
00:00:18.380 --> 00:00:21.590
that might understand certain things

9
00:00:21.590 --> 00:00:24.760
that I have experienced, and felt, and gone through.

10
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.150
And this is all about you,

11
00:00:27.150 --> 00:00:32.150
but I just can't tell you what a safe space you're in,

12
00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:37.170
what an amazing talent and incredible force you are.

13
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.900
I just wanna talk girl to girl, woman to woman,

14
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:47.900
I think in our weird trajectory of life you grow up so fast

15
00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:52.550
and you're in these adult jobs

16
00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:55.780
and yet you're not an adult,

17
00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.130
but you're hanging out with other kids sometimes

18
00:00:59.130 --> 00:01:02.390
and working with them, but your peers aren't kids and-

19
00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:03.223
Yes.

20
00:01:03.223 --> 00:01:05.570
And what your responsibilities are

21
00:01:05.570 --> 00:01:07.790
and what you have access to.

22
00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.200
Yes, definitely!

23
00:01:09.200 --> 00:01:11.470
Like you're the one paying the rent,

24
00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:12.880
so it's like really weird.

25
00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:14.303
I always rebelled against authority

26
00:01:14.303 --> 00:01:16.147
'cause deep down inside, I was like,

27
00:01:16.147 --> 00:01:17.820
"I'm paying the rent around here."

28
00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.360 line:15%
My parents, they did the best that they could.

29
00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:25.570 line:15%
There's no manual on how to raise a child star.

30
00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:30.570 line:15%
And when the child star retorts back after the parent says,

31
00:01:30.967 --> 00:01:33.340
"You know, you're grounded for sneaking out

32
00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:35.147
at three in the morning, whatever."

33
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:41.910
I retorted with, "Well, I pay the bills,

34
00:01:41.910 --> 00:01:43.130
what are you gonna do?

35
00:01:43.130 --> 00:01:44.920
What are you gonna do to keep me grounded?"

36
00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:46.420
And so it was challenging.

37
00:01:46.420 --> 00:01:49.993
And it was also really, really interesting.

38
00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:54.680
I started working on set at eight years old

39
00:01:54.680 --> 00:01:56.217
on "Barney and Friends".

40
00:01:58.250 --> 00:02:02.210
And so I was around adults at eight years old,

41
00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.560
and I actually always got along better with adults

42
00:02:05.560 --> 00:02:07.130
than kids.

43
00:02:07.130 --> 00:02:09.620
For some reason that was just always me.

44
00:02:09.620 --> 00:02:11.850
And so when I got around a lot of adults

45
00:02:11.850 --> 00:02:14.350
like I felt more comfortable.

46
00:02:14.350 --> 00:02:18.110
But the adults leave work and they go and have a drink,

47
00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:20.410
the kids, what do we do?

48
00:02:20.410 --> 00:02:23.500 line:15%
So it was this weird thing to think about,

49
00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:26.650 line:15%
especially then when you become a teenager in Hollywood,

50
00:02:26.650 --> 00:02:31.650 line:15%
and your adult peers are, like I said,

51
00:02:31.760 --> 00:02:34.440 line:15%
going to a bar after work or whatever,

52
00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.653
and you're 17 thinking,

53
00:02:38.647 --> 00:02:41.748
"Well, what do I get to do to play?"

54
00:02:41.748 --> 00:02:44.630
And it left this weird...

55
00:02:46.090 --> 00:02:47.300
I have this mentality of like,

56
00:02:47.300 --> 00:02:48.980
if you're gonna work me like an adult,

57
00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:50.870
I get to party like one.

58
00:02:50.870 --> 00:02:54.936
But the reality was, adults weren't partying like I was, so.

59
00:02:54.936 --> 00:02:55.769
Yeah.

60
00:02:55.769 --> 00:02:56.700
No, but it's true.

61
00:02:56.700 --> 00:03:01.660
I remember I always wanted to be the person

62
00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.640
who had a martini at the dinner party

63
00:03:04.640 --> 00:03:06.900
and was getting a cocktail,

64
00:03:06.900 --> 00:03:10.214
but I'm the girl who probably just drinks too many martinis

65
00:03:10.214 --> 00:03:11.553
at the dinner party.

66
00:03:11.553 --> 00:03:13.030
(Demi laughing)

67
00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:15.260
It's like, okay, let's just start earlier

68
00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:20.230
because when you have to put the good, the bad,

69
00:03:20.230 --> 00:03:24.000
and the ugly out there, even at a ripe young age,

70
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.100
I don't understand why the press

71
00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:30.940
feels like they have the right to ask you anything.

72
00:03:30.940 --> 00:03:34.090
And why we also feel like the people pleasers

73
00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.290
who have to answer all their questions.

74
00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:39.570
Yes, yes, yes, totally, totally.

75
00:03:39.570 --> 00:03:42.130
I've battled with that a lot,

76
00:03:42.130 --> 00:03:47.130
even in the first few interviews after,

77
00:03:47.920 --> 00:03:50.533
or getting ready for the documentary to come out.

78
00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:55.670
I was answering questions that I realized,

79
00:03:55.670 --> 00:03:58.180
I was like, I don't have to answer these.

80
00:03:58.180 --> 00:03:59.790
And so we do...

81
00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:02.920
I like to say that I'm an open book with boundaries,

82
00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:07.150
because I feel like I do wear my heart on my sleeve,

83
00:04:07.150 --> 00:04:10.970
but I also have learned how to protect myself

84
00:04:10.970 --> 00:04:15.970
from people intruding a little bit too much

85
00:04:17.467 --> 00:04:19.570
on the personal stuff.

86
00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:23.480
Well, I got really angry when that guy on the red carpet,

87
00:04:23.480 --> 00:04:26.300
I think it's that gentleman, Chris, whatever his name is,

88
00:04:26.300 --> 00:04:29.470
I forget his last name, Chris Harrison.

89
00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:32.680
And he's a funny guy and he's out there working,

90
00:04:32.680 --> 00:04:36.430
and this is no offense to him, it could have been anybody,

91
00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:39.117
it's not personal for him, but when he says,

92
00:04:39.117 --> 00:04:41.330
"You're 16, what do you know about heartbreak?"

93
00:04:41.330 --> 00:04:43.960
Which isn't the worst question that's been asked-

94
00:04:43.960 --> 00:04:45.090
Totally!
But I think

95
00:04:45.090 --> 00:04:48.450
because the narrative the documentary has just revealed

96
00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:51.750
that you had been through so much by 16,

97
00:04:51.750 --> 00:04:53.310
I wanted to rip his face off.

98
00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:56.830
Why does he have the right to say that to you?

99
00:04:56.830 --> 00:05:00.400
You know, I think that the reason why we get angry

100
00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:02.070
when we watch that clip back

101
00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:05.350
is because we're looking at a 16-year-old girl

102
00:05:05.350 --> 00:05:07.090
answering the question.

103
00:05:07.090 --> 00:05:12.027
It's really easy for us to look at a teenager

104
00:05:12.027 --> 00:05:14.510
and feel empathy for her.

105
00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:16.560
I definitely have...

106
00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.150
I think that's so interesting

107
00:05:19.150 --> 00:05:20.810
the way you're speaking about it.

108
00:05:20.810 --> 00:05:24.010
It's very high road, it feels very zen and calm.

109
00:05:24.010 --> 00:05:27.740
I still have the angry little rebel in me

110
00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.010
that no matter how open I am,

111
00:05:30.010 --> 00:05:35.010
I think when entitled, quippy questions come out

112
00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:39.200
I still harken back to times in my life where I'm like,

113
00:05:39.200 --> 00:05:43.480
I wish I had had the ability to say, gosh, you know,

114
00:05:43.480 --> 00:05:46.410
I've been so open, but I'm gonna have to say,

115
00:05:46.410 --> 00:05:47.918
not gonna answer that.

116
00:05:47.918 --> 00:05:49.290
Absolutely.

117
00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:51.466
And at some point you have to say,

118
00:05:51.466 --> 00:05:52.830
all right, I'm putting my foot down,

119
00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:56.420
and I gotta say, no, I can't answer these questions anymore.

120
00:05:56.420 --> 00:05:58.520
And I think it's never too late

121
00:05:58.520 --> 00:06:00.790
to set a boundary with the world.

122
00:06:00.790 --> 00:06:02.923
And I learned that,

123
00:06:04.279 --> 00:06:07.850
and I've continued to set boundaries where,

124
00:06:07.850 --> 00:06:12.390
I know it seems like I've talked a lot about my life,

125
00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:14.610
but I didn't talk about everything.

126
00:06:14.610 --> 00:06:17.303
And that's where my boundary is.

127
00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:20.010
There's things I'll never share with the world

128
00:06:20.010 --> 00:06:22.253
because no one could benefit from them.

129
00:06:23.353 --> 00:06:25.330
And that's okay.

130
00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:27.360
That's for me and my treatment team.

131
00:06:27.360 --> 00:06:31.220
I love that because it's sexy, it's mysterious, it's fun,

132
00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:33.890
it makes people wanna know more.

133
00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:35.740
It makes people think, "Oh my God,

134
00:06:35.740 --> 00:06:37.580
there's even more to this person

135
00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:41.050
than they've already been so gracious to let me in about."

136
00:06:41.050 --> 00:06:44.130
That being said, how do you feel

137
00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:47.823
now that you have put that out there in the documentary?

138
00:06:48.880 --> 00:06:53.880
Well, so I actually feel like it's just easier

139
00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:58.070
to be transparent and honest with people

140
00:06:58.070 --> 00:07:01.290
because I couldn't... I don't know, it's just,

141
00:07:01.290 --> 00:07:04.500
there's nothing less appealing to me

142
00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:09.290
than pretending something didn't happen when it did,

143
00:07:09.290 --> 00:07:12.550
and then living a lie trying to pretend that way like that.

144
00:07:12.550 --> 00:07:14.830
That's not appealing to me whatsoever,

145
00:07:14.830 --> 00:07:17.840
and I've just never been that kind of person anyway.

146
00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:22.630
So I feel like it was sometimes in a way

147
00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:25.330
like my only option,

148
00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:28.270
but not in a sense where I felt obligated.

149
00:07:28.270 --> 00:07:32.600
It was just, it was the only thing that felt good to me.

150
00:07:32.600 --> 00:07:33.433
Absolutely.

151
00:07:33.433 --> 00:07:34.880
And yeah.

152
00:07:34.880 --> 00:07:37.463 line:15%
(upbeat music)

